The order is left subtree, right subtree, then the current node. A small helper keeps adding values only after both recursive calls finish.

This is easy to follow, but a very tall tree can make the recursion stack large.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> postorder(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        visit(root, result);
        return result;
    }

    private void visit(TreeNode node, List<Integer> result) {
        if (node == null) {
            return;
        }

        visit(node.left, result);
        visit(node.right, result);
        result.add(node.data);
    }
}
```

For a tree with root `1`, left child `2`, and right child `3`, the calls finish in this order: `2`, `3`, then `1`. The result is `[2, 3, 1]`.

An empty tree returns an empty list, and a single node returns a list containing that node.

Time complexity: `O(n)`, since every node is visited once.

Space complexity: `O(h)` for the call stack, where `h` is the tree height. The result list uses `O(n)` space.

Instead of waiting for a node until both children are done, this version records nodes in the order root, right, left. Reversing that list gives left, right, root, which is the needed order.

The push order matters. The left child is pushed first so the right child is removed first from the stack.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> postorder(TreeNode* root) {
        vector<int> result;
        if (root == nullptr) {
            return result;
        }

        stack<TreeNode*> nodes;
        nodes.push(root);

        while (!nodes.empty()) {
            TreeNode* current = nodes.top();
            nodes.pop();
            result.push_back(current->data);

            if (current->left != nullptr) {
                nodes.push(current->left);
            }
            if (current->right != nullptr) {
                nodes.push(current->right);
            }
        }

        reverse(result.begin(), result.end());
        return result;
    }
};
```

The input tree is not changed. The extra stack and the temporary result both can grow with the number of nodes.

Time complexity: `O(n)`, including the final reverse.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the stack and result vector.

A single stack is enough if it also remembers the last completed node. When the node on top has an unvisited right child, that child is explored first. Otherwise, both sides are finished and the node can be added.

This does not alter the tree. The pointer comparison is what stops the traversal from visiting the same right subtree again.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> postorder(TreeNode* root) {
        vector<int> result;
        stack<TreeNode*> nodes;
        TreeNode* current = root;
        TreeNode* lastVisited = nullptr;

        while (current != nullptr || !nodes.empty()) {
            while (current != nullptr) {
                nodes.push(current);
                current = current->left;
            }

            TreeNode* top = nodes.top();
            if (top->right != nullptr && lastVisited != top->right) {
                current = top->right;
            } else {
                result.push_back(top->data);
                lastVisited = top;
                nodes.pop();
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

The empty tree is handled naturally because both `current` and the stack start empty. A tree with only right children also works because each right child is explored before its parent is recorded.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(h)` for the stack, with `O(n)` additional space for the output.

